{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>אֲדֹנִי אַל נָא תָשֵׁת וְגוֹ׳</b> — from Aaron's words it seems that Moses was offended by their words, and therefore he pleads with him to forgive them their sin. And it appears that Aaron's reasoning was that he judged Moses by the standard of a sage, for according to the law (Kiddushin 32a) a sage who forgives his honor — his honor is forgiven. Therefore he estimated in his mind that if he would forgive, the affliction would depart. And he said <b>אַל נָא תָשֵׁת</b> etc., meaning: even though you were offended by the words to the point that the punishment came, now that the punishment has come the iniquity is atoned and you should no longer lay sin upon us. But according to the truth it is not so, for Moses was not offended by the matter, and therefore Scripture acted wisely and adjacent to their speaking about him it stated, ״וְהָאִישׁ מֹשֶׁה עָנָו מְאֹד״, intending to negate Aaron's words here." "And the reason for the punishment is for two reasons: the first is that we must judge Moses by the standard of a king, as it is written (Deuteronomy 33:5) ״וַיְהִי בִישֻׁרוּן מֶלֶךְ״, and a king who forgives his honor — his honor is not forgiven (ibid.). And perhaps this is also what HaShem intended in His statement to them, <b>מַדּוּעַ לֹא יְרֵאתֶם לְדַבֵּר בְּעַבְדִּי</b>, for the kingship belongs to HaShem, and the servant of a king is [like] a king (Shavuot 47a)." "<b>And a second reason:</b> according to what I explained, that HaShem was offended because they suspected Moses of having done wrong, heaven forbid, and HaShem agreed with him and did not rebuke them — if so, this is a matter on the supernal side, and therefore He punished them even though there is no offense taken by Moses here. And Moses was vindicated, for he was not the cause of the offense that required him to pray to HaShem; and even so, it did not avail to heal her until seven days had passed — whereas if the matter had depended on him, he would not have needed prayer but would simply forgive and the affliction would depart. From this you learn that the punishment came for one of the two reasons, or for both together, and it is possible that had Moses been offended by their words the punishment would have come in another manner, doubly and redoubled upon them, heaven forbid." "<b>אֲשֶׁר נוֹאַלְנוּ וַאֲשֶׁר וְגוֹ׳</b> — the intent is for two matters, according to what I explained regarding HaShem's indignation against them: that He was offended because they equated their prophecy with the prophecy of Moses, and He was offended because they infringed upon the honor of Moses by saying ״לֹא טוֹב עָשָׂה.״ Therefore, corresponding to the equating of their prophecy to his prophecy, he said <b>אֲשֶׁר נוֹאַלְנוּ</b>, for they spoke words of foolishness in this; and corresponding to what they said about him, ״לֹא טוֹב עָשָׂה,״ he said <b>וַאֲשֶׁר חָטָאנוּ.</b>"], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:08:20.171011431Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}